/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Proposition began the debate with the argument that instilling a sense of patriotism in our younger generation through the promotion of a social-science-based curriculum could potentially help children foster a deep bond with national heritage and culture. The first speaker emphasized that a pivotal part of creating patriotism and encouraging students to be innovative in their approaches to problems we face today, is by ensuring that students comprehend the enormity of national historical struggles, such as the Indian fight for independence. This was reiterated by the second speaker, who claimed that for the "holistic development of future leaders", exposure to the topics covered in social science, such as democratic ideals, and civic sense  is absolutely pivotal. The Proposition conveyed how learning about our predecessors and overcoming dilemmas and social injustices would be helpful in motivating children thereby helping them attain a thoughtful mindset in solving the problems the world faces today.                )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
