/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Proposition articulated with a poignant example of a young boy aspiring to play a sport but unable to do so due to the absence of facilities in his area. The team argued that enhancing access would allow more individuals to explore their potential in sports, which is not solely about victory or defeat but also about instilling values such as teamwork, perseverance, and discipline. They then reinforced that providing access to passionate individuals naturally fosters quality. Conclusively, their innate talent and determination outweigh the need for lavish infrastructure, asserting that no amount of equipment can compensate for the absence of skill.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Opposition eloquently argued that millions already enjoy access, yet only a fraction succeed due to the lack of quality facilities, making quality the true priority. The team further expanded on this by stating that in rural regions, where even basic infrastructure is absent, building sport academies would be futile; instead, the quality of well-functioning urban centres should be improved. Although the Opposition agreed that access is relevant, they also insisted that in the current era, excellence in quality ultimately determines success, emphasizing that accessibility without skill refinement leads to wasted potential.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
