504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In response, the Opposition painted the motion as regressive and exclusionary, warning that it could reintroduce dangerous norms like white supremacy by setting rigid ethnic boundaries. They argued that casting should be based on talent, not skin color, and questioned whether enforcing ethnic alignment sacrifices storytelling. Highlighting the symbolic importance of casting, they pointed to milestones like the first Black Disney princess as moments that uplifted entire communities. Forcing ethnic exclusivity, they contended, could unintentionally stereotype actors and undermine diverse talent. Furthermore, they warned of backlash from marginalised communities and insisted that society has evolved past such rigid identity lines. They reinforced the potential of discrimination in the motion, and claimed that there are far more stories with white leads, as opposed to other races, thereby reducing opportunity for minorities.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Both sides raised essential questions about the balance between representation and artistic freedom, and how best to promote inclusivity without reducing individuals to their ethnic labels, however, ultimately, it was the Proposition who swayed the judges, winning the round with a compelling case for how alignment between identity and portrayal can empower audiences, enrich narratives, and reshape the film industry for the better.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
