/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In a charged semi-final clash, the debate between Proposition and Opposition unfolded over the motion: This House believes that important characters in movies should only be portrayed by actors of the same ethnicity. As the room filled with arguments that cut across culture, creativity, and commerce, this round marked a powerful intersection between representation and artistic freedom.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
525 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Proposition opened with an emotionally grounded stance, urging the audience to imagine a world where actors must perform roles that compromise their personal beliefs and cultural integrity. They argued that casting actors of a different ethnicity risks inaccurate portrayal, mental distress, and viewer offense; all of which harm the authenticity and reception of the film. The team emphasized that for key roles, especially those rooted in cultural or religious identities, only those who share the lived experience can do justice to the performance. They further highlighted that such casting improves the mental well-being of actors, strengthens minority representation, and fosters inclusivity in an industry dominated by majority narratives.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
